---
/**
 * ServiceWound — the concrete case, before any framework. From Analytical Governance v2.0 §1 + §10.
 *
 * THE NUMBERS ARE v2.0's, AND THEY ARE NOT THE v1.1 NUMBERS (CG2 final E rulings, 2026-08-26 15:59, item 7). This is
 * the finding that made the whole historical route necessary, so it is written down here:
 *
 *             v1.1 (r02 §1, §8)                     v2.0 (r03 §1, §10)
 *   roster    50 stores were OPEN yesterday         50 stores EXIST
 *   open      —                                     48, per an operating-status source
 *   revenue   47 ordinary observations              47 revenue rows
 *   detail    2 governed zero-revenue days,         none
 *             1 feed failure
 *   options   47 / 49 / 50                          47 / 48 / 50
 *
 * The v1.1 component also carried a ruled disposition — the 50-store population IS the governed
 * population, and the requested result is withheld because one store-day lacks evidence. Under v2.0
 * that sentence has no support: 50 is the roster, and the roster does not establish openness. So this
 * is a NEW component rather than an edit, and the v1.1 one is preserved unchanged at
 * /history/analytical-governance-v1-1 (components/ag-v1-1/OpenStoreWound.astro).
 *
 * STILL NO CROWNED NUMBER, for the v2.0 reason rather than the v1.1 one: what the three facts
 * establish is not yet what any of them may serve, and the serving vocabulary is typed later. A page
 * that quietly crowned a denominator would teach that governance is about picking the right one.
 *
 * THE WORD "CORRECT" IS QUALIFIED EVERYWHERE IT APPEARS (Huayin, final E rulings, 2026-08-26 15:59, item 3). Not
 * "correct number" — "arithmetically flawless", "executes correctly", and the predicate that is
 * actually being denied: not established as the legitimate answer to the user's purpose.
 */
const FACTS = [
  {
    n: '47',
    what: 'revenue rows arrived',
    establishes: 'what reported — not what was open',
    source: 'the sales table',
  },
  {
    n: '50',
    what: 'stores exist',
    establishes: 'the roster — not that 50 were open',
    source: 'the store roster',
  },
  {
    n: '48',
    what: 'were open yesterday',
    establishes: 'the requested population',
    source: 'the operating-status source',
  },
];
---

<section id="the-wound" class="wound ds ds-paper ds-section" aria-labelledby="wound-h">
  <div class="ds-measure-wide">
    <h2 id="wound-h" class="ds-label">A number the database can produce and the service cannot yet serve</h2>

    <p class="question">What was average revenue per open store yesterday?</p>

    <dl class="facts">
      {FACTS.map((f) => (
        <div class="fact" id={`fact-${f.n}`}>
          <dt><span class="num">{f.n}</span> {f.what}</dt>
          <dd class="est">{f.establishes}</dd>
          <dd class="src">{f.source}</dd>
        </div>
      ))}
    </dl>

    <div class="landing">
      <p class="ds-body">
        The arithmetic can divide total observed revenue by 47, by 48, or by 50. Each of the three can
        be written in valid SQL, each executes correctly, and each returns a plausible number.
        <strong>The computation does not tell us which answer serves the user&rsquo;s purpose.</strong>
      </p>
      <p class="ds-body">
        The 50-store roster does not establish that all 50 were open. The 47 revenue rows do not
        establish the open-store population. A system that returns the mean over reporting stores
        while presenting it as <em>average revenue per open store</em> can be arithmetically flawless
        and unfaithful as a service &mdash; and a system that identifies the right population but uses
        stale or incomplete state can be faithful in interpretation and unreliable in production.
      </p>
      <p class="disposition">
        No number is entitled to answer this question yet, and the reason is not arithmetic. The
        population has to be established, the state that population requires has to be shown to be
        present, and only then is there a question about what may be served.
      </p>
      <p class="disposition-note ds-body">
        Establishing the population and establishing a servable answer are two different things, and
        only the first is in reach here.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .question {
    margin: var(--ds-space-3) 0 var(--ds-space-5);
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: var(--ds-display);
    line-height: 1.12;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    max-width: 30ch;
    text-wrap: balance;
  }

  .facts { margin: 0; display: grid; gap: 0; }
  .fact {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 22ch) minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 18ch);
    gap: 0.4rem 2rem;
    align-items: baseline;
    padding: 0.9rem 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--ds-rule);
  }
  .fact:last-child { border-bottom: 1px solid var(--ds-rule); }
  .fact dt { font-family: var(--font-serif); font-size: 1rem; color: var(--ink); margin: 0; }
  .fact .num { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 1.05rem; margin-right: 0.35rem; }
  .fact dd { margin: 0; font-family: var(--font-serif); font-size: 0.98rem; line-height: 1.5; }
  .fact .est { color: var(--ink); }
  .fact .src { font-family: var(--font-sans); font-size: var(--ds-meta); color: var(--ink-faint); }

  .landing { margin-top: var(--ds-space-5); display: grid; gap: var(--ds-space-3); max-width: var(--ds-essay); }
  .landing .ds-body strong { color: var(--ink); }
  .disposition {
    margin: 0;
    padding-left: 0.9rem;
    border-left: 2px solid var(--ink);
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: var(--ds-lead);
    line-height: 1.45;
    color: var(--ink);
  }
  .disposition-note { color: var(--ink-soft); }

  @media (max-width: 52rem) {
    .fact { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); gap: 0.2rem; }
    .fact .src { padding-top: 0.15rem; }
  }
</style>
